      "quirks": [
        "Rowan County probate/estate files (1753-1970) are the primary source for a father's death when no death certificate exists (North Carolina did not require statewide civil death registration until 1913). A father's estate could be probated any time from a child's infancy to roughly 60 years after the child's birth — size the search window to the FATHER's plausible lifespan, not the child's own research window.",
        "Rowan County probate index (1753-1900) is full-text searchable but only partially name-indexed (~40%) — keyword search for the surname across the whole 1839-1899 window is more reliable than browsing by year."
      ],
      "guide_markdown": "# Locality Guide: Rowan County, North Carolina (1839-1899)\n\nNo statewide civil death registration until 1913 — probate/estate files (1753-1970) are the primary substitute. A father's estate could be probated any time from his child's infancy to roughly 60 years after the child's birth; size the search window to the father's plausible lifespan, not the child's own.",
